Linked List Theory
===================

What Linked List Actually Is:
-----------------------------
- Nodes connected by pointers
- Each node stores value + reference to next (and prev if doubly)
- No contiguous memory, no index access


Types and When to Use Each:
----------------------------

Singly Linked List:
  - Only need forward traversal
  - Insert/delete at head frequently
  - Problems: reverse, merge, detect cycle, find middle
  - Real use: simple stack, hash chaining

Doubly Linked List:
  - Need both forward AND backward traversal
  - Need to delete a node when you already have its reference
  - Real use: LRU cache, undo/redo (step by 1), browser back/forward (step by 1), text editor cursor

Circular Linked List:
  - Data is naturally cyclic
  - Real use: round robin scheduling, turn-based games, Josephus problem, looping playlist


5 Situations Where Linked List is the RIGHT Choice:
-----------------------------------------------------

1. Frequent insert/delete at head
   - Array insert at head -- O(n) shift everything
   - Linked list -- O(1) just change pointer

2. Frequent insert/delete in middle WITH node reference
   - You already have pointer to the node
   - DLL delete -- O(1) just relink prev and next
   - Array delete -- O(n) shift everything

3. Problem IS about pointer manipulation
   - Reverse linked list
   - Merge two sorted lists
   - Reorder odd/even nodes
   - Swap pairs
   - The problem literally gives you a linked list

4. LRU Cache (DLL + HashMap)
   - Need O(1) access by key -- hashmap
   - Need O(1) move to front / remove least recent -- DLL
   - No other data structure combo does both in O(1)

5. Unknown size, frequent structural changes
   - Don't know how many elements ahead of time
   - Elements constantly entering and leaving from random positions
   - No need for index access


When NOT to Use Linked List:
-----------------------------

1. Need index access
   - "give me element at position 5"
   - Array -- O(1)
   - Linked list -- O(n) walk from head

2. Need to search for a value
   - "does 42 exist?"
   - Hashset -- O(1)
   - Linked list -- O(n) scan entire list

3. Counting/frequency
   - "how many times does 'a' appear?"
   - Hashmap -- O(1) lookup
   - Linked list -- useless here

4. Subarray/substring/window problems
   - Sliding window needs contiguous index-based access
   - Linked list can't do windows

5. Sorting
   - Array sort -- O(n log n) clean
   - Linked list sort -- O(n log n) but messy, cache unfriendly

6. Simple stack/queue
   - Just append/pop from ends
   - Array or deque does this better
   - Linked list has pointer overhead for no gain

7. Jump by multiple steps
   - "go back 5 steps"
   - Array -- O(1) ptr -= 5
   - Linked list -- O(5) walk 5 nodes


Linked List vs Everything Else:
--------------------------------
I need to...                        |  Use
------------------------------------|---------------------------
Access by index                     |  Array
Check existence                     |  Hashset
Count frequency                     |  Hashmap
LIFO push/pop                       |  Stack (array)
FIFO enqueue/dequeue                |  Deque
Find pairs in sorted data           |  Two pointers on array
Window/subarray                     |  Sliding window on array
Search sorted data                  |  Binary search on array
Insert/delete at head fast          |  Singly linked list
Insert/delete middle with ref       |  Doubly linked list
LRU cache                           |  DLL + hashmap
Manipulate node connections         |  Linked list
Cyclic round robin                  |  Circular linked list
Undo + redo step by 1              |  DLL
Undo + redo jump by steps          |  Array + pointer
Version branching                   |  Tree


Interview Pattern Recognition:
-------------------------------

Problem gives you a linked list node?
  -> It's a linked list problem, use pointer manipulation

Problem says "design" with ordered insert/delete?
  -> Probably DLL + hashmap (LRU style)

Problem says "maintain order" + frequent middle changes?
  -> Linked list

Everything else?
  -> Probably not linked list -- array/hashmap/stack/deque is almost always better


One Liner:
----------
- Linked list = structure manipulation (changing connections)
- Array = value manipulation (reading, computing, windowing)
- If the problem is about what the data IS -- use array/hashmap
- If the problem is about how nodes CONNECT -- use linked list
